# Byte-exact fixtures: two local gates compare these files byte-for-byte against
# generated output (`key_normalization_fixture` in packages/core, `check-policy-census.sh`).
# Their committed blobs are LF; without pinning, Windows autocrlf checks the working tree
# out as CRLF and both gates false-fail locally while Linux CI stays green. Pin instead of
# repo-wide `* text=auto` to keep this a targeted fix (no mass renormalization).
# The convention-vocab baseline is regenerated the same way (`--update-baseline` rewrites it
# whole and the guard greps it line-exact), so it needs the same pin.
# The detection-benchmark coverage floor is the same shape again: benchmark.mjs rewrites it whole with
# `\n` on every --update-baseline, so without the pin a Windows working tree turns every line into a
# spurious CRLF diff the first time the floor is raised.
# The DSL inline-value census is the same shape a third time: `zzop_core::dsl::inline_census_tests`
# rewrites it WHOLE from the packs on every regeneration, writing `\n`. Without the pin, the first
# regeneration on the other platform turns all ~650 lines into a spurious diff.
scripts/policy-census.txt text eol=lf
scripts/convention-vocab-baseline.txt text eol=lf
scripts/detection-expected-baseline.txt text eol=lf
scripts/dsl-inline-census.txt text eol=lf
docs/adapters/*.fixture.json text eol=lf

# The plugin's SessionStart hook, pinned for the same reason in a nastier place: Claude Code clones
# this repo as a marketplace onto the USER's machine, and on Windows (autocrlf on by default in Git
# for Windows) that checkout would rewrite the script as CRLF. A `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` shebang is
# the classic "bad interpreter" failure, on exactly the platform where this hook is already the most
# fragile — and the hook was verified working as LF, so pin what was tested.
.claude-plugin/hooks/*.sh text eol=lf

# The two generated site editions, same shape a fourth time and for a guard that runs on EVERY commit:
# scripts/gen-site.mjs writes them with `\n`, they are committed (Pages serves site/ as files), and
# scripts/check-site-generated.sh regenerates and compares them byte-for-byte. Without the pin, a
# Windows checkout (autocrlf on by default) hands that guard a CRLF copy of an LF blob and it reports
# the whole file as drift on a tree that has none — a pre-commit guard that cries wolf on a fresh clone
# is a guard that gets commented out. Pinned narrowly rather than `site/*.html`: site/graph.html and
# site/rules.html are checked out CRLF today, and gen-site.mjs deliberately flattens the former's line
# endings when it slices the dep-graph block out of it.
site/index.html text eol=lf
site/ko/index.html text eol=lf

# The generator's INPUTS need the same pin, and this half is easier to miss than the half above.
# Sentences in site-src/content/*.mjs are multi-line template literals: a CRLF working copy puts a
# literal `\r` INSIDE each string, so the generator faithfully writes CRLF into pages whose committed
# blobs are LF -- and check-site-generated.sh then reports drift that no edit caused. Pinning only the
# outputs would leave the guard red on a fresh Windows clone for a reason nobody could find by reading
# the diff. site-v2.css rides in through readFileSync and lands in the same `<style>` block, so it is
# the same case. (scripts/gen-site.mjs and scripts/site/render.mjs are NOT pinned: their line endings
# never reach the output.)
site-src/content/*.mjs text eol=lf
site-src/site-v2.css text eol=lf

# Shell scripts, and this one is not a false-red story like the four above -- it is a deploy that
# already failed (2026-08-14). ONE guard was committed with CRLF and CI died on `$'\r': command not
# found` before the site or the release could ship. Nothing local could have caught it: Git for
# Windows' bash tolerates CRLF in a script, so all 39 guards ran green here while the same bytes were
# unparseable on the Linux runner. That asymmetry is the whole problem -- the platform that authors
# the file is the one platform that cannot feel the break. A guard would only re-measure what the
# checkout already decides, so the fix belongs here, where CRLF cannot enter the blob in the first
# place. `.githooks/*` is listed separately because it holds no extension.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
